e internal struggle  for regional security superiority among  two players Saudi Arabia  and Iran neither of which is exactly  operating according to American values  which begs the question the Saudi Arabia  even deserve US military assistance in a  situation like this well I don't think  the us-saudi relationship is really  built on shared values  I think it's built on a certain sense of  what our interests are that could be  improved if we were moving beyond a  fossil fuel economy I think it's also  the case that as Saudi Arabia tests the  limits of our friendship for example  murdering a journalist who is a u.s.  resident there needs to be some kind of  consequences for that now for the  current president this seems to just be  in dollars and cents relationship to me  it is about American security and it is  about American values and there should  be some sense that we will enforce our  values when they are challenged by a  country that's supposed to be our ally  let alone what we see happening across  the Gulf from a player like Iran if you  were at the United Nations General  Assembly this week as president and you  could meet with only four other world  leaders who would they be well I think  it's time to strengthen our  relationships with European allies and  so either Angela Merkel or manual  McCallum who in many ways I think is  emerging as a kind of successor  generation leader in Central Europe I  think we need to shore that up and have  that conversation so that's one or two  yeah well we'll do 1a and 1b okay we  need to engage with Iran now I wouldn't  do it without some kind of sense that  there was momentum but if our respective  diplomatic relationships could produce  even the smallest incremental  trust-building measure I think that  would justify a conversation about how  we could cause tensions in that region  to go down rather than up that would  require lifting sanctions on Iran II and  leaders so you'd be open to doing that  cuz they say they wouldn't meet with  Americans unless that happens I think we  could find a way if the focus of US  policy were to de-escalate rather than  ratchet up tension oh that's - yeah  we've got 1a 1b in doing  I would like to have a conversation with  the new leader of El Salvador someone  who is I think actually a little bit  younger than I asked great and  represents a part of the country of the  world Central America where we have a  lot at stake first of all the Central  American triangle Guatemala Honduras and  in El Salvador  our countries whose instability is  contributing to issues at our own border  and also countries that are seeking to  stabilize and improve what's happening  there they should have a friend in our  hemisphere in the United States and I'd  like to have a dialogue about how to get  that going okay you got one more well I  think we'd better increase the rate of  conversation we're having with Xi  Jinping the China challenge is going to  be one of the dominant issues for the  United States in my lifetime as I  mentioned at the debate the president  said he would like to see me negotiate  with Xi Jinping I'd like to see him  produce a deal with Xi Jinping but since  it hasn't happened I think we need to  have a very frank conversation about  ways in which we will adjust the future  of the relationship and that may include  an orderly decoupling of some of our  technological interdependencies because  they have very different values they're  developing their technology according to  very different values including  different ideas about surveillance and  the relationship technologically that we  have could become a u.s. security  vulnerability this is not just about  trade this is about technology security  and stability I heard you say on the bus  yesterday you're working on your final  budget proposal back home in South Bend  the other issue of course that through a  lot of your attention drew a lot of  attention from across the country over  the summer was the situation with the  police shooting it's been about three  months what's how has that situation  evolved and in your view or things in a  better place in your city than they were  then you know the pain that we went  through as a community as a consequence  of that situation has led to a very  constructive dialogue about the future  of policing in the community it's  prompted us to look at new ways to give  community members of voice in the way  policy decisions are made  everything from the use of force to the  use of body-worn cameras and even as  information is still being gathered in  the outside investigation of that  particular incident we know that we  didn't have to wait in order to act as a  community and work to build trust there  will continue to be major challenges  around race and policing in our city and  I think in any diverse urban city in  America and it's all the more reason why  we need not only local leadership to  deal with this but also a national  environment that helps reverse the  disempowerment of communities of color  and especially when it comes to police  the relationship with black Americans  that's partly about policing but also  largely about deep questions of  institutional racism that impact housing  health and economic opportunity you're  still struggling to build support among  black and Latino voters in this country  if they get asked about you or they see  you and they think oh you know  interesting that he's young interesting  that he's a military veteran but you  know you know white guy he doesn't  understand our situation and there was a  police shooting in his town just like  there isn't every other scene in this  country I don't know there's got to be  somebody better than that what's your  argument to them why should they be  looking at you right now as a potential  president first of all I've put forward  the most comprehensive plan of any  candidate on dealing with systemic  racism in this country and it's  motivated not just by the conversations  I've been having as a candidate but by  the experience of leading a diverse city  that has these challenges as a  consequence not only of local dynamics  but what's going on around the country  it won't get fixed without national  leadership and our plans about  everything from increasing  entrepreneurship in the black community  to making sure we deliver equity and  health and put an end things like excuse  me the maternal mortality yeah are very  well received because they are among the  most comprehensive ideas put forward on  this issue the other thing that I think  is important is for all of us who have  experienced some kind of exclusion to be  allies at a time like this  each candidate brings a different  experience in a different story  forward I don't have the experience of  being a black American I don't have the  experience of talking to a teenage son  about what to do when you were pulled  over by a police officer I can assume if  I am walking in a department store and  I'm fidgeting with something in my  pocket that I will be given the benefit  of the doubt and not followed I don't  have those personal experiences but I do  have the experience of belonging to a  group that has been feared hated and  denied opportunity in this country and  while those aren't the same it's one of  the reasons I'm powerfully motivated to  ensure that nobody is denied opportunity  in this country because of who they are  you are a gay man you are now ranked at  least fourth in Iowa and you're doing  pretty well nationally has the history  or the historic nature of what you're  doing set in yet it's certainly moving  when people of different ages come up to  me at campaign events  high schoolers who let me know that I've  made it easier for them to get through  their day people my parents age with  tears in their eyes who did not think  something like this was ever going to be  possible in their lifetime and so I'm  very conscious that that there's a  historic quality to this campaign and at  the same time it's important to me that  no one element of my story defined me  this is part of who I am just like the  fact that I'm a veteran and the fact  that I'm a mayor the fact that I'm young  and the fact that I come from northern  Indiana and taken together they add up  to a story that I hope explains why I'm  motivated to lead America into a new  sense of belonging as well as a better  set of policies for our future
 

